  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "There are NNN islands, numbered 111 to NNN.\n\nInitially, every pair of islands is connected by a bridge. Hence, there are a total of N⋅(N−1)2\\frac{N \\cdot (N - 1)}{2}2N⋅(N−1)​ bridges. The importance of the ithi^{th}ith island is AiA_iAi​.\n\nEverule lives on island 111 and enjoys visiting the other islands using bridges. The cost of destroying the bridge between island iii and jjj is Ai⋅AjA_i \\cdot A_jAi​⋅Aj​.\n\nDominater has the power to destroy any number of bridges such that the **sum of costs** of destroying the bridges is **at most** CCC.\n\nFind the **minimum** number of islands (including island 111) that Everule can visit if Dominater destroys bridges optimally.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nThe first line of input will contain a single integer TTT, denoting the number of test cases.\nEach test case consists of multiple lines of input.\n\nThe first line of each test case contains two space-separated integers NNN and CCC — the number of islands and upper bound on the total cost of destroying bridges.\nThe second line contains NNN space-separated integers, A1,A2,…,ANA_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_NA1​,A2​,…,AN​, denoting the importance of each island.\n\n\n\n\nOutput Format\nFor each test case, output on a new line, the **minimum** number of islands (including island 111)  Everule can visit.",
    "constraints": [
      "1≤T≤1001 \\le T \\le 1001≤T≤100",
      "1≤N≤1001 \\le N \\le 1001≤N≤100",
      "1≤Ai≤1071 \\le A_i \\le 10^71≤Ai​≤107",
      "1≤C≤10181 \\le C \\le 10^{18}1≤C≤1018"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "4\n3 1000000000000000000\n2000000 4000000 5000000\n4 150\n100 1 1 1\n6 1275\n35 15 10 25 10 5\n6 400\n35 15 10 25 10 5",
        "output": "1\n3\n4\n6",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest Case 1 : The array AAA representing the importances of the cities is [2⋅106,4⋅106,5⋅106][2 \\cdot 10^6, 4 \\cdot 10^6, 5 \\cdot 10^6][2⋅106,4⋅106,5⋅106] and the value of CCC is 101810^{18}1018.\n\nThe cost of destroying bridge between island 111 and 222 is A1⋅A2=8⋅1012A_1 \\cdot A_2 = 8 \\cdot 10^{12}A1​⋅A2​=8⋅1012\nThe cost of destroying bridge between island 222 and 333 is 20⋅101220 \\cdot 10^{12}20⋅1012\nThe cost of destroying bridge between island 111 and 333 is 10⋅101210 \\cdot 10^{12}10⋅1012\n\nThe total sum of costs of destroying all the bridges is only 38⋅101238 \\cdot 10^{12}38⋅1012, and since C=1018C = 10^{18}C=1018, we can destroy all the bridges as their cost doesn't exceed CCC. This makes Everule unable to visit any island other than 111.\nTest Case 2 : Destroy the following bridges :\n\nIslands 111 and 222 : cost is 100100100\nIslands 222 and 333 : cost is 111\nIslands 222 and 444 : cost is 111\nIslands 333 and 444 : cost is 111.\n\nThe total cost is 103103103 which is less than C=150C = 150C=150. Now, we can visit only islands 111, 333 and 444 from island 111, since the bridges between 111 and 333, and 111 and 444 have not been destroyed yet. Thus, the answer is 333."
      }
    ]
  },
